      {
        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:cacf6cbd-2116-47c9-97f4-e7a1994f45d9",
        "expected": "I have worked most with LLM systems where the hard part is deciding whether the output should be trusted. At Intelehealth I designed an evaluation framework for AI-generated clinical outputs against a 4,169-case benchmark, using stratified and statistical sampling to surface systematic failure modes rather than a single aggregate score. In tSearch I built a rubric-driven LLM judging system where individual judges evaluate separate dimensions in parallel and then synthesize an evidence-cited assessment, and where missing evidence returns \"insufficient evidence\" instead of a low score. On the retrieval side, I built Gloria's memory layer with OpenAI embeddings, Pinecone vector search, and a Neo4j graph, and at Summer Atlantic Capital I built a retrieval-augmented explanation layer over an anomaly-detection model so analysts get a readable reason for an alert. I have also done straight modeling work: temporal sequence models over event streams with RNN and Transformer-style approaches, learning-to-rank for alert prioritization, XGBoost with SHAP interpretation on a roughly 10 million record survey dataset, and a PyTorch CNN for facial-landmark prediction at about 95% PCK. For agents, I use Playwright with OpenAI only at narrow call sites.",
        "observed": "I have worked most with LLM systems where the hard part is deciding whether the output should be trusted. At Intelehealth I designed an evaluation framework for AI-generated clinical outputs against a 4,169-case benchmark, using stratified and statistical sampling to surface systematic failure modes rather than a single aggregate score. In tSearch I built a rubric-driven LLM judging system where individual judges evaluate separate dimensions in parallel and then synthesize an evidence-cited assessment, and where missing evidence returns \"insufficient evidence\" instead of a low score. On the retrieval side, I built Gloria's memory layer with OpenAI embeddings, Pinecone vector search, and a Neo4j graph, and at Summer Atlantic Capital I built a retrieval-augmented explanation layer over an anomaly-detection model so analysts get a readable reason for an alert. I have also done straight modeling work: temporal sequence models over event streams with RNN and Transformer-style approaches, learning-to-rank for alert prioritization, XGBoost with SHAP interpretation on a roughly 10 million record survey dataset, and a PyTorch CNN for facial-landmark prediction at about 95% PCK. For agents, I use Playwright with OpenAI only at narrow call sites.",
        "match": true
      },
      {
        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:48f39a6c-29c7-4c62-ab12-f2d948f59ce2",
        "expected": "Notion is describing itself as a workspace where teams and agents think together, and that is the specific problem I have spent the last two years building around. In my job-application agent I deliberately built a deterministic, operator-controlled system instead of a general autonomous browser agent: every application moves through an explicit state machine, every mutating capability sits behind fail-closed flags like SUBMIT_ENABLED, and free-text and demographic fields are architecturally prevented from being auto-filled because a wrong action there is not recoverable. I also built a validation ladder so a capability is not called working until it has been tested against the level of reality it claims to support. That is the question I would want to work on at Notion: where an agent should act inside someone's real workspace, where it should stop and hand back to the person, and how you actually prove the boundary holds. I also care about shipping things people use. I built ClarityAtlas end to end in Swift and SwiftUI and put it into a live Hopkins pilot with 30+ users, and the feedback loop from real users changed the product more than any design doc did.",
        "observed": "Notion is describing itself as a workspace where teams and agents think together, and that is the specific problem I have spent the last two years building around. In my job-application agent I deliberately built a deterministic, operator-controlled system instead of a general autonomous browser agent: every application moves through an explicit state machine, every mutating capability sits behind fail-closed flags like SUBMIT_ENABLED, and free-text and demographic fields are architecturally prevented from being auto-filled because a wrong action there is not recoverable. I also built a validation ladder so a capability is not called working until it has been tested against the level of reality it claims to support. That is the question I would want to work on at Notion: where an agent should act inside someone's real workspace, where it should stop and hand back to the person, and how you actually prove the boundary holds. I also care about shipping things people use. I built ClarityAtlas end to end in Swift and SwiftUI and put it into a live Hopkins pilot with 30+ users, and the feedback loop from real users changed the product more than any design doc did.",
